Yes—many people can intentionally evoke that experience, and it’s a combination of several mechanisms rather than a property of the spiral alone.

When you look at a spiral:

* Microsaccades (tiny involuntary eye movements) continually shift the image across your retina.
* Neural adaptation causes some visual neurons to respond less over time than others, creating changing patterns of activity.
* Depth interpretation kicks in because spirals are compatible with cues for expansion, contraction, tunnels, vortices, shells, and rotation. Your visual system is constantly choosing among plausible 3D interpretations.
* Top-down expectation matters too. Once you imagine it rotating inward or outward, your brain can stabilize that interpretation for a while, much like the way the Necker cube flips between two equally valid 3D orientations or the spinning dancer illusion appears to reverse direction.

Your thought about the “deltas” is interesting. Binocular vision certainly contributes to depth perception in general, but even with one eye closed many spiral illusions still produce a vivid sense of motion or depth. That suggests the effect is distributed across several cues rather than coming solely from stereoscopic vision.

In a way, the spiral is an underconstrained signal. Your visual system is trying to infer the most likely 3D process from a 2D pattern, and because multiple interpretations fit, your brain can “run the simulation.” The animation is an active inference, not something literally present in the image.

